     1	# RKT Simulator — Specification
     2	
     3	## 1. Overview
     4	
     5	RKT Simulator is a browser game, built with Three.js, that simulates launching
     6	model rockets (Estes-style) into a variety of procedurally generated
     7	environments. The player picks a rocket, a motor, and an environment, then
     8	launches and watches the flight play out with live telemetry. Flights can go
     9	nominally or fail in physically-motivated (and occasionally funny) ways.
    10	
    11	**Design pillars**
    12	
    13	- **Grounded-but-approachable physics.** Real-ish thrust curves, mass, drag,
    14	  gravity, wind, and parachute recovery — tuned for readable, fun flights, not
    15	  certification-grade accuracy.
    16	- **Pure simulation core.** The physics/sim layer imports no Three.js and is
    17	  fully unit-testable. Rendering and UI sit on top and only read sim state.
    18	- **Clean, minimal, not over-engineered.** Small focused modules with clear
    19	  interfaces. YAGNI applied ruthlessly.
    20	
    21	## 2. Goals and non-goals
    22	
    23	### Goals
    24	
    25	- Simulate a curated set of ~12 recognizable Estes-style rockets.
    26	- Simulate a variety of seeded, procedurally generated environments (realistic
    27	  and a few funny ones).
    28	- Provide live telemetry (altitude, velocity, apogee, flight status).
    29	- Provide a light per-launch challenge/scoring layer on top of a sandbox.
    30	- Run smoothly in a desktop browser with a cohesive low-poly art style.
    31	- Be built test-first, with a pure sim core covered by unit tests.
    32	
    33	### Non-goals (explicitly out of scope)
    34	
    35	- Live scraping of estesrockets.com (specs are hand-authored, offline).
    36	- High-fidelity aerospace simulation (RASP `.eng` files, staging, clusters,
    37	  barometric atmosphere, RK4).
    38	- Career/progression, persistence, unlockables, or a save system.
    39	- Multiplayer or networking.
    40	- PBR/realistic art or an external asset pipeline.
    41	- Mobile-first / touch-optimized controls (mobile may work but is not targeted).
    42	
    43	## 3. Target platform
    44	
    45	- Desktop browsers (current Chromium/Firefox). Mouse + keyboard.
    46	- Delivered as a Vite app: `npm run dev` for local play, `npm run build` for a
    47	  static bundle.
    48	
    49	## 4. Gameplay
    50	
    51	### 4.1 Core loop (sandbox)
    52	
    53	1. Player selects a **rocket**, a compatible **motor**, and an **environment**.
    54	2. Player presses **Launch**.
    55	3. The simulation runs; the camera follows the rocket; the HUD shows live
    56	   telemetry.
    57	4. The flight reaches an outcome (landed or failed). A summary is shown
    58	   (apogee, flight time, outcome, drift distance).
    59	5. Player resets and launches again (same or new selection).
    60	
    61	### 4.2 Challenges (light objectives)
    62	
    63	- Optional per-launch challenge overlay. Two challenge types at launch:
    64	  - **Target altitude:** get apogee within a tolerance band of a target.
    65	  - **Landing zone:** land the recovered rocket inside a marked ground zone.
    66	- A score is computed for the launch (0–100), shown in the summary. Challenges
    67	  are opt-in and stateless — no cross-session progression.
    68	
    69	### 4.3 Controls
    70	
    71	- Mouse drag / scroll: orbit + zoom the camera (OrbitControls).
    72	- Toggle **follow camera** (tracks the rocket) vs **free orbit**.
    73	- Launch, Reset, and Mute buttons in the UI.
    74	- Keyboard: `Space` = launch/reset, `C` = toggle camera mode, `M` = mute.
    75	
    76	## 5. Rockets
    77	
    78	A curated subset of ~12 Estes-style models, hand-authored in a local data file.
    79	Names and specs approximate real models; they are not scraped and are treated as
    80	plausible game data, not authoritative product data.
    81	
    82	Each rocket record:
    83	
    84	| Field            | Type      | Meaning                                              |
    85	|------------------|-----------|------------------------------------------------------|
    86	| `id`             | string    | Stable identifier                                    |
    87	| `name`          | string    | Display name                                         |
    88	| `massEmptyKg`     | number    | Dry mass (no motor)                                  |
    89	| `diameterM`      | number    | Body tube diameter → reference area for drag         |
    90	| `dragCoefficient`| number    | Cd (dimensionless)                                   |
    91	| `chuteDiameterM` | number    | Parachute canopy diameter (0 = streamer/tumble)      |
    92	| `chuteCd`        | number    | Parachute drag coefficient                           |
    93	| `recommendedMotors` | string[] | Motor ids that fit this rocket                     |
    94	| `maxMotorImpulse` | number   | Total impulse (N·s) above which CATO risk rises      |
    95	| `look`           | object    | Low-poly mesh params (body length, fin shape, colors)|
    96	
    97	Initial roster (approximate, subject to tuning): Alpha, Wizard, Big Bertha,
    98	Der Red Max, V2, Baby Bertha, Hi-Flier, Crossfire ISX, Mean Machine,
    99	Photon Probe, Nike Smoke, Star Orbiter. Final list authored in `data/rockets.ts`.
   100	
   101	## 6. Motors
   102	
   103	Estes motor classes A–E, hand-authored. Each motor record:
   104	
   105	| Field           | Type    | Meaning                                        |
   106	|-----------------|---------|------------------------------------------------|
   107	| `id`            | string  | e.g. `A8-3`, `C6-5`, `E12-4`                    |
   108	| `class`        | string  | `A`..`E`                                        |
   109	| `totalImpulseNs`| number  | Total impulse (N·s)                             |
   110	| `avgThrustN`    | number  | Average thrust (N)                             |
   111	| `burnTimeS`     | number  | Propellant burn duration (s)                   |
   112	| `massTotalKg`   | number  | Loaded motor mass                              |
   113	| `massPropKg`    | number  | Propellant mass (burned off during boost)      |
   114	| `delayS`        | number  | Ejection delay after burnout                    |
   115	
   116	### 6.1 Thrust curve
   117	
   118	A parametric piecewise curve generated from `avgThrustN` and `burnTimeS`: a fast
   119	rise to a peak, a sustain, and a taper to zero. The curve is scaled so that its
   120	time integral equals `totalImpulseNs` (impulse conservation is a hard invariant,
   121	enforced by a unit test). This avoids shipping `.eng` files while keeping the
   122	boost phase physically sensible.
   123	
   124	## 7. Physics model
   125	
   126	Single rigid point-mass with vertical-plus-lateral motion (2D in the vertical
   127	plane of flight is sufficient; represented as 3D vectors for rendering). Fixed
   128	timestep integration, decoupled from render frame rate via an accumulator.
   129	
   130	Forces each step:
   131	
   132	- **Thrust:** from the thrust curve during boost, along the rocket axis.
   133	- **Gravity:** `m·g`, `g = 9.81 m/s²`.
   134	- **Drag:** `½·ρ·v²·Cd·A`, opposing velocity. `A` from body diameter during
   135	  ascent/coast; from parachute (`chuteDiameterM`, `chuteCd`) during recovery.
   136	- **Wind:** a horizontal wind vector (constant + gust component) applied through
   137	  drag, producing lateral drift.
   138	
   139	Mass decreases linearly with burned propellant during boost. Air density `ρ`
   140	from a simple altitude model in `atmosphere.ts` (does not need to be barometric).
   141	
   142	Integration: **semi-implicit (symplectic) Euler** at a fixed `dt` (e.g. 1/120 s).
   143	Chosen over RK4 for simplicity and stability at this fidelity.
   144	
   145	### 7.1 Flight state machine (`flight.ts`)
   146	
   147	```
   148	idle → boost → coast → apogee → descent → landed
   149	                                   ↘ (failure) → failed
   150	```
   151	
   152	- **idle:** on pad, awaiting launch.
   153	- **boost:** motor burning, thrust > 0.
   154	- **coast:** burnout to apogee (vertical velocity > 0).
   155	- **apogee:** vertical velocity crosses zero → trigger recovery (chute deploy
   156	  after ejection delay).
   157	- **descent:** falling under parachute (or ballistic if chute failed).
   158	- **landed:** vertical position returns to ground height.
   159	- **failed:** a failure outcome ended the flight early.
   160	
   161	### 7.2 Outcomes (`outcomes.ts`)
   162	
   163	Condition- and probability-driven, using the seeded RNG:
   164	
   165	- **nominal:** normal flight and recovery.
   166	- **CATO** (catastrophe at takeoff / motor explosion): raised probability when
   167	  the chosen motor's impulse exceeds the rocket's `maxMotorImpulse`. Ends flight
   168	  early with an explosion effect near the pad/low altitude.
   169	- **chute-fail:** parachute fails to deploy at apogee → ballistic descent (hard
   170	  landing). Probability small but non-zero, higher for tumble-recovery rockets.
   171	- **tip-off:** low thrust-to-weight at launch (or high wind) causes an unstable,
   172	  angled trajectory off the rail.
   173	
   174	Outcome selection happens at defined decision points (ignition, apogee) and is
   175	fully deterministic given the seed, so it is unit-testable.
   176	
   177	## 8. Environments
   178	
   179	Each environment is a **seeded generator** implementing a common interface. Given
   180	a seed, it produces:
   181	
   182	- A **scene contribution:** terrain mesh + props (Three.js), built in `world/`.
   183	- A **params bundle** (pure data, no Three.js): ground height at origin, wind
   184	  profile (base speed/direction + gust), world bounds, and an optional target
   185	  landing zone for challenges.
   186	
   187	Because the params bundle is pure data derived deterministically from the seed,
   188	the environment generator's *logic* is unit-testable without rendering.
   189	
   190	Environment interface (conceptual):
   191	
   192	```ts
   193	interface Environment {
   194	  readonly id: string;
   195	  readonly name: string;
   196	  generate(seed: number): EnvironmentInstance;
   197	}
   198	
   199	interface EnvironmentInstance {
   200	  params: EnvParams;              // pure data (testable)
   201	  build(scene: THREE.Scene): void; // adds meshes/props (rendering only)
   202	}
   203	```
   204	
   205	### 8.1 Environment set
   206	
   207	Realistic: **park, urban, mountain, desert, sea, rooftop of a house.**
   208	Funny (at least two): e.g. **giant bathtub** (launch from a rubber duck) and
   209	**backyard with an angry dog** (chases the landing). Final funny set decided
   210	during implementation; the architecture supports adding more cheaply.
   211	
   212	Each environment supplies its own wind character (e.g. sea = stronger steady
   213	wind; urban = gusty turbulence between buildings; desert = light and hot). Wind
   214	is owned by the environment's `params` (there is no separate wind field on
   215	`SimConfig`); the config's `seed` deterministically drives the gust component.
   216	
   217	## 9. Rendering (`world/`)
   218	
   219	- **Style:** low-poly, flat/gradient shading, cohesive palette. No textures/PBR.
   220	- **Rocket mesh:** generated procedurally from the rocket's `look` params
   221	  (body cylinder, nose cone, fins, colors) in `rocketMesh.ts`.
   222	- **Effects:** particle-ish flame/smoke during boost, parachute canopy on
   223	  recovery, explosion burst on CATO/hard landing. Kept simple (sprites or small
   224	  meshes), not a full particle engine.
   225	- **Camera:** OrbitControls for free look; a follow-cam mode that tracks the
   226	  rocket smoothly. Toggle between them.
   227	- **Loop:** `requestAnimationFrame` drives rendering; a fixed-step accumulator
   228	  advances the simulation independently so physics is frame-rate independent.
   229	
   230	## 10. UI (`ui/`)
   231	
   232	DOM overlay (not in-canvas) for simplicity:
   233	
   234	- Rocket selector (with a small spec readout), motor selector (filtered to the
   235	  rocket's compatible motors), environment selector.
   236	- Launch / Reset / Mute buttons; camera-mode toggle.
   237	- Live HUD: altitude, vertical velocity, current apogee, flight status/phase.
   238	- Post-flight summary: apogee, max velocity, flight time, outcome, drift
   239	  distance, and (if a challenge was active) the score.
   240	- Optional challenge panel (choose challenge type + parameters before launch).
   241	
   242	## 11. Audio (`audio/`)
   243	
   244	Lightweight SFX, **muted by default**, with a mute toggle: launch roar, whoosh,
   245	chute pop, explosion. Small/synthesized sounds; kept off the critical path
   246	(failure to load audio never blocks a launch). No music.
   247	
   248	## 12. Determinism and RNG
   249	
   250	A single seeded RNG (`mulberry32`) underpins procedural generation and outcome
   251	selection. Given the same `SimConfig` (including seed), a full flight is
   252	reproducible. This is the backbone of the test strategy.
   253	
   254	## 13. Data flow
   255	
   256	```
   257	UI  ──builds──▶  SimConfig { rocket, motor, environment, seed, challenge? }
   258	                     │
   259	                     ▼
   260	             Simulation (pure sim/) ──fixed-step──▶ FlightState + Telemetry
   261	                     │                                     │
   262	       reads (no write-back)                       reads (no write-back)
   263	                     ▼                                     ▼
   264	             Renderer (world/)                        HUD (ui/)
   265	```
   266	
   267	`world/` and `ui/` never mutate `sim/` state; they only read it. This one-way
   268	dependency is a hard architectural rule.
   269	
   270	## 14. Testing strategy
   271	
   272	Test-first (TDD) for the entire pure `sim/` layer, using **Vitest**:
   273	
   274	- **thrustCurve:** integral equals `totalImpulseNs` within tolerance; shape
   275	  (rise/sustain/taper) and non-negativity.
   276	- **integrator:** apogee for a known rocket+motor lands within a tolerance band
   277	  of a hand-computed expected value; energy/behavior sanity (goes up then down).
   278	- **atmosphere:** density decreases with altitude; wind vector composition.
   279	- **flight:** state-machine transitions occur in the correct order and on the
   280	  right triggers.
   281	- **outcomes:** deterministic given seed; CATO probability rises with motor
   282	  overload; chute-fail path yields ballistic descent.
   283	- **rng:** deterministic sequence for a fixed seed.
   284	- **environments:** params bundle is deterministic per seed; wind/bounds/target
   285	  zone within expected ranges.
   286	- **challenge scoring:** correct score for known apogee/landing inputs and
   287	  boundary cases.
   288	
   289	**Rendering/UI** are not unit-tested. They are smoke-tested via **Brave CDP**
   290	(remote debugging on port 9222): canvas mounts, a launch runs end-to-end, no
   291	console errors, telemetry values update during flight, screenshot for visual
   292	sanity.
   293	
   294	**Quality gate (must pass before "done"):**
   295	`vitest run` (all pass) + `tsc --noEmit` (no type errors) + `vite build`
   296	(builds clean). A `make`-style unified target or npm script runs all three.
   297	
   298	## 15. Tech stack
   299	
   300	- **Three.js** (npm) for rendering.
   301	- **TypeScript** for the whole codebase.
   302	- **Vite** for dev server and build.
   303	- **Vitest** for unit tests.
   304	- No backend; fully static output.
   305	
   306	## 16. Success criteria
   307	
   308	- Player can select from ~12 rockets, compatible motors, and multiple
   309	  procedurally generated environments, and launch.
   310	- Flights show plausible boost/coast/apogee/recovery behavior with live
   311	  telemetry and at least the four outcome types.
   312	- The pure `sim/` layer is covered by passing unit tests, including impulse
   313	  conservation, apogee tolerance, state-machine order, and determinism.
   314	- CDP smoke test confirms the app runs end-to-end with no console errors.
   315	- `vitest` + `tsc --noEmit` + `vite build` all pass.
   316	- Code is organized per the module layout with the one-way `sim/ → world/,ui/`
   317	  dependency respected.

